# Do monopolies drive innovation?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | curious, open-minded economist host who loves a puzzle; warm, thinks aloud, brisk lively pace (never slow, never rushed); takes objections seriously, never preachy","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; brisk lively pace, never slow"}
prompt: "Why did Schumpeter think big firms with profits innovate most? Static versus dynamic efficiency: which matters more over decades?"
next: {"title":"Why do airlines charge different prices for the same seat?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/econ-from-zero/36-why-do-airlines-charge-different-prices.cast"}

## A lab inside a monopoly
use: timeline
with:
    view:
      from: 1940
      to: 1990
    eras:
      - {from: 1940, to: 1984, label: AT&T monopoly, color: '#b5482e'}
    events:
      - {id: transistor, date: 1947, label: Transistor, priority: 1, cites: [gertner]}
      - {id: shannon, date: 1948, label: Information theory, sublabel: Shannon, priority: 1, cites: [gertner]}
      - {id: solar, date: 1954, label: Solar cell, priority: 1, cites: [gertner]}
      - {id: unix, date: 1969, label: Unix, priority: 1, cites: [gertner]}
      - {id: breakup, date: 1984, label: Broken up, priority: 1}
sources:
    - id: gertner
      title: 'The Idea Factory: Bell Labs and the Great Age of American Innovation'
      authors: Jon Gertner
      year: 2012
      finding: Bell Labs, funded by AT&T's regulated telephone monopoly, produced the transistor (1947), Shannon's information theory (1948), the silicon solar cell (1954) and Unix (1969); AT&T was broken up in 1984.

    icon lab_icon size 90 set twemoji x 660 y 560 icon_look picture of microscope hidden true
    text lab_txt "Bell Labs" cites ["gertner"] x 800 y 560 font_size 40 hidden true
    text dwl "Textbook: monopoly = deadweight loss" #b5482e x 500 y 170 font_size 30 hidden true
    text trade "today's triangle → tomorrow's inventions?" x 500 y 170 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "A lab inside a monopoly"

Do monopolies hold back new inventions, or drive them? Start with a firm that had almost no rivals at all.
    draw axis

B: AT&T. For most of the twentieth ce (@draw era_0@)ntury it ran nearly every telepho (@draw event_breakup@)ne line in the United States.

    highlight era_0 duration 1.5

Out of those monopoly profits it paid  (@draw lab_icon lab_txt parallel true@)for Bell Labs, a research lab in New Jersey. Look what came out of it.

In 1947, the transistor: the tiny switch inside every computer chip.
    draw event_transistor

B: In 1948, Claude Shannon's information theory, the mathematics behind every bit we send.
    draw event_shannon

Then in 195 (@draw event_solar@)4 the first practical silicon solar cell, and in 1969 Unix (@draw event_unix@), whose ideas still run most servers.

B: That jars with the textbook. A monopoly ch (@draw dwl@)arges too much, sells too little, and leaves a deadweight loss.

It does. Yet one of the most inventive labs of the century sat inside a monopoly.
    highlight events

    quiz "Where do you expect more innovation?" judge false intro "Your guess first." voice b
        right: Keep that guess. The evidence comes at the end.
        * Many small firms competing hard
        * One big firm with fat profits
        * Somewhere in between

    erase dwl

So the question is real: the pro (@draw trade@)fits that cost us a triangle today might pay for the inventions of tomorrow.

    pause 0.5

## Schumpeter's bet
sources:
    - id: csd
      title: Capitalism, Socialism and Democracy
      authors: Joseph A. Schumpeter
      year: 1942
      finding: Innovation by large firms, financed by monopoly profits, drives capitalist progress through 'creative destruction'; 'perfect competition is not only impossible but inferior' (p. 106).

    portrait js x 860 y 430 width 190 of "Joseph Schumpeter" cameo true hidden true
    box profit "Profit" x 420 y 615 width 250 height 85 radius 10 icon "money bag" font_size 34 hidden true
    box research "Research" x 680 y 460 width 250 height 85 radius 10 icon microscope font_size 34 hidden true
    box newprod "New product" x 420 y 305 width 270 height 85 radius 10 icon "light bulb" font_size 34 hidden true
    box tempmono "Temporary monopoly" x 165 y 460 width 270 height 85 radius 10 font_size 32 hidden true
    arrow e1 profit -> research curved hidden true
    arrow e2 research -> newprod curved hidden true
    arrow e3 newprod -> tempmono curved hidden true
    arrow e4 tempmono -> profit curved hidden true
    box rival "Rival's new idea" #b5482e x 150 y 305 width 230 height 80 radius 10 icon "light bulb" font_size 28 style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    arrow hit rival -> tempmono style.stroke #b5482e hidden true
    text cd "creative\ndestruction" #b5482e x 420 y 460 font_size 34 hidden true
    source book cites ["csd"] x 895 y 545 width 150 of "Capitalism, Socialism and Democracy" hidden true
    text quote "“not only impossible\nbut inferior”" cites ["csd"] x 870 y 270 font_size 28 hidden true

    card title "Schumpeter's bet"

B: The man who bet on the (@draw js@) big firm was Joseph Schumpeter, an Austrian-born economist writing at Harvard in 1942.

B: His point: the competition that matters isn't a lower price. It's th (@draw newprod@)e new product or method that makes the old one obsolete.

    erase js

And why would that fa (@draw profit@)vour a big firm with no rivals?

B: Research is costly and risky, and banks dislike lending on a (@draw e1 research@)n idea. Profits pay for it without asking anyone.

B: And t (@draw e2@)he inventor must keep the rew (@draw e3 tempmono@)ard. If rivals can copy you the next morning, why spend a cent?

So the hope of a monopoly is the bait.  (@draw e4@)The profit is the prize for inventing.

    highlight tempmono duration 1.5

B: Exactly. And the prize never lasts. A (@draw rival hit@) rival's new idea tears it down. He called that  (@draw cd@)creative destruction.

So a few years of high prices could be the price of progress.
    flow e1 e2 e3 e4

    erase cd

B: He p (@draw book@)ut it bluntly: perfect competition is, quote,  (@draw quote@)not only impossible but inferior.

    erase book quote

So the profit the textbook counts as waste is, for Schumpeter, the fuel of the engine.
    highlight profit effect circle

    pause 0.5

## Static versus dynamic
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation:
      - C(t) = 100*(1 + g/100)^t
      - M(t) = (100 - L)*(1 + (g + d)/100)^t + 0*E
    params:
      g: {value: 1, fixed: true}
      L: {value: 2, fixed: true}
      d: {value: 0, min: 0, max: 1, step: 0.05, label: extra growth d}
      E: {value: 10, fixed: true}
    editable:
      - d
    drag: d
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 10
    y_range:
      - 96
      - 112
    x_label: Years
    y_label: Town output
    marks:
      - {kind: point, id: cross, at: 'min(E, ln(100/(100 - L))/ln((100 + g + d)/(100 + g)))', curve: 0, label: false, guides: true}
      - {kind: point, id: endc, at: E, curve: 0, label: '164', guides: false}
      - {kind: point, id: endm, at: E, curve: 1, label: '206', guides: false}
    box:
      x: 10
      'y': 140
      w: 980
      h: 640

    text stat "static: −2%" #b5482e at.data [6.5,100.5] font_size 32 hidden true
    text dyn "dynamic: +0.5 a year" #b5482e x 380 y 510 font_size 32 hidden true
    cards mathq options [{"text":"Show me the math","goto":"math"},{"text":"Skip it","goto":"test"}] then test hidden true
    scratch mth work [{"tex":"\\text{catch-up years} \\approx L \\div d"},{"tex":"2 \\div 0.5 = 4"}] x 380 y 480 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Static versus dynamic"

    hide equations

To weigh the two arguments, follow both futures, first over ten years. Take Harbourtown's boatyard, with illustrative numbers.
    draw axes

B: Future one: many small yards compete, (@draw curve_0@) boats sell at cost, and the town's output starts at 100 and grows one percent a year.

Future two: the boatyard is a mo (@draw curve_1@)nopoly. Its deadweight loss leaves the town two percent poorer from day one.

B: That gap is static (@draw stat@) efficiency: how well the town uses what it already has. On that score the monopoly loses every year.

But say its profits pay for better hulls, and the town grows one and a half percent a year instead of one.
    highlight curve_1

B: My guess? Making up two percent takes a generation.
    highlight stat

    quiz "The monopoly town starts 2% poorer but grows 1.5% a year instead of 1%. How long until it is ahead?" intro "What's yours?"
        right: About four years: half a point a year eats a two percent gap fast.
        wrong: A gap of half a point a year closes a two percent hole quickly: about four years.
        + About 4 years
        * About 40 years
        * Never

    erase stat

About four years.
    animate params.d 0.5 duration 2

    draw cross

After fifty, the monopoly town has 206 (@draw endc endm@) against 164.
    animate x_range.1 50 y_range.0 90 y_range.1 215 params.E 50 duration 2.5

B: So the slope is dynam (@draw dyn@)ic efficiency: how fast what you have gets better. Over decades, slopes beat levels.

    highlight curve_1 duration 2

    erase dyn endc endm

Your turn: drag the extra growth down toward zero and watch the crossing year run away.
    explore

B: At zero it never catches up. Then the monopoly is all cost and no gain.
    animate params.d 0 duration 2.5

    erase cross

    draw mathq

    ask "Show the math, or skip it?" on mathq stage own voice b
        intro: Want the rule of thumb behind that catch-up time?

@math

    erase mathq

A rule of thumb: the years to catch up are roughly the static loss divided by the extra growth.
    draw mth_box mth_line_1

B: Two percent over half a point a year: four years. Halve the extra growth and it takes twice as long.
    draw mth_line_2

    erase mth

@test

    erase mathq

(@draw cross@) So a market can lose on today's scorecard and still win over a lifetime, but only if its profits really buy faster progress.
    animate params.d 0.5 duration 2

    highlight curve_1 cross duration 2

## The lazy monopolist
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Schumpeter and Arrow would both hire you.
    good: Ask what the profit pays for, and who is coming to take it.
    poor: Two scorecards take a while. Try the slope, not the level.
    none: The race is long; there is time.
domain: {"x":[0,10],"y":[0,10]}
sources:
    - id: arrow1962
      title: Economic Welfare and the Allocation of Resources for Invention
      authors: Kenneth J. Arrow
      year: 1962
      finding: A monopolist gains less from a cost-reducing invention than a competitive firm, because the new profit partly replaces its existing monopoly profit (the replacement effect).
    - id: kodak
      title: This man invented the digital camera in 1975 — and his bosses at Kodak never let it see the light of day
      authors: Business Insider
      year: 2015
      finding: Kodak engineer Steve Sasson built the first digital camera in 1975; Kodak, dependent on film, kept it as an R&D project; Kodak filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy on 19 January 2012.
      url: https://www.businessinsider.in/tech/this-man-invented-the-digital-camera-in-1975-and-his-bosses-at-kodak-never-let-it-see-the-light-of-day/articleshow/48520882.cms
    - id: aghion2005
      title: 'Competition and Innovation: An Inverted-U Relationship'
      authors: Aghion, Bloom, Blundell, Griffith & Howitt
      year: 2005
      finding: 'In UK industry panel data (citation-weighted patents, Lerner index), innovation first rises then falls with product-market competition: an inverted U.'
      doi: 10.1093/qje/120.2.701
    - id: hashmi2013
      title: 'Competition and Innovation: The Inverted-U Relationship Revisited'
      authors: Aamir Rafique Hashmi
      year: 2013
      finding: With US publicly traded manufacturing firms, a mildly negative relationship between competition (inverse markups) and citation-weighted patents; no inverted U.
      doi: 10.1162/REST_a_00364

    text why "Why hurry?" #b5482e x 500 y 450 font_size 64 hidden true
    portrait ka x 860 y 450 width 190 of "Kenneth Arrow" cameo true hidden true
    scratch kodak cites ["kodak"] work ["Kodak: digital camera, 1975","lived off film","bankrupt, 2012"] x 430 y 420 font_size 44 hidden true
    text repl "replacement effect" #2f6b8f cites ["arrow1962"] x 450 y 230 font_size 48 hidden true
    axes ax x_label Competition y_label Innovation hidden true
    curve hump expr "8.5 - 0.3*(x - 5)^2" x_from 0.6 x_to 9.4 cites ["aghion2005"] hidden true
    label uk "UK data" above attach_to hump hidden true
    curve us dashed #8f887c expr "5.2 - 0.15*x" x_from 0.6 x_to 9.4 cites ["hashmi2013"] hidden true
    label usl "US data" below attach_to us hidden true
    text lab_arrow "Arrow:\nlazy monopolist" #2f6b8f at.data [1.5,8.7] font_size 40 hidden true
    text lab_schump "Schumpeter:\nno reward" #b5482e at.data [8.5,8.7] font_size 40 hidden true
    text stamp "contested" #b5482e at.data [5,2] font_size 52 hidden true
    text sc1 "Static: how well we slice today's pie" #b5482e x 500 y 400 font_size 54 hidden true
    text sc2 "Dynamic: how fast the pie grows" #2f6b8f x 500 y 300 font_size 54 hidden true
    icon pie size 150 set twemoji x 500 y 550 icon_look picture of pie hidden true

    card title "The lazy monopolist"

B: That 'only if' is where I push back. W (@draw why@)hy would a safe monopoly hurry at all?

The economis (@draw ka@)t Kenneth Arrow asked just that in 1962. A monopolist with a better product mostly replaces its own profit.

    erase why

B: Like Ko (@draw kodak_box kodak_line_1@)dak. Its engineer Steve Sasson built a digital camera in 1975.

    erase ka

B: Kodak, living off film, was slow to sell it,
    draw kodak_line_2

B: and went bankrupt in 2012.
    draw kodak_line_3

While a newcomer, with no profit to lose, gains the whole prize. That (@draw repl@)'s Arrow's replacement effect.

    erase kodak repl

B: So who's right, Schumpeter or Arrow?
    draw ax

Maybe both. In British industry dat (@draw hump uk@)a, Philippe Aghion and coauthors found innovation peaks at middling competition.

With too little compe (@draw lab_arrow@)tition, the safe monopolist dozes, as Arrow said.

B: With too much, nobody (@draw lab_schump@) keeps enough of the reward, as Schumpeter said.

B: But with America (@draw us usl@)n data, Aamir Hashmi found no hump, just a mild slope down. So how sure are we? Co (@draw stamp@)ntested.

Schumpeter's ca (@highlight lab_schump@)se is strong where monopolies are temporary prizes, as with patents and big labs. It (@highlight lab_arrow@) breaks down where a monopoly is safe from any rival.

So do monopolies drive innovation? A prize for inventors helps; a monopoly with nothing to fear mostly drives itself.
    highlight hump

    ask "A postal service protected by law from every rival: which side of the hump is it on? Tap it." choose ["lab_arrow","lab_schump"] answer lab_arrow stage own
        intro: Three new cases. First: a monopoly no rival can ever enter.
        right: Arrow's side: with no rival to fear, standing still costs it nothing.
        wrong: What does it lose by standing still? Nothing: it sits on Arrow's side.

    erase ax hump uk us usl lab_arrow lab_schump stamp

    draw pie sc1 sc2 parallel true

    quiz "A patent lets an inventor charge far above cost for twenty years. What is the law trading?" intro "Second, patents." voice b
        right: Lower prices today for faster innovation later: a little static efficiency is given up to buy dynamic efficiency.
        wrong: Ask what the high price costs now, and what it pays for: lower prices today are traded for faster innovation later.
        + Lower prices today for faster innovation later
        * Faster innovation today for lower prices later
        * Nothing: a patent removes the deadweight loss

    quiz "A monopoly costs 5% of output today and adds 0.1 percentage points of growth a year. Roughly how long until it pays off?" intro "Last one, with numbers." voice b
        right: About 50 years: five percent over a tenth of a point a year. A small growth edge takes a lifetime to repay a big static loss.
        wrong: Divide the static loss by the extra growth: five over a tenth is about fifty years.
        * About 5 years
        + About 50 years
        * About 500 years

B: Which is why asking whether markets work needs two s (@highlight sc1@)corecards: how well we slice today's pie, (@highlight sc2@) and how fast it grows.

    pause 0.5

